  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B003",
        "laureates": [
          "Alexander B. Zamolodchikov",
          "Alexander Polyakov",
          "John Cardy"
        ],
        "citation": "for foundational conformal-field-theory methods revealing universal behavior at two-dimensional critical points"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B059",
    "B013"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B003 remains my preferred achievement. It identified a compact and extraordinarily generative description of criticality: symmetry and locality constrain the long-distance physics so strongly that exact, universal results become possible. This is not merely a useful formalism; it changed how statistical physicists connect microscopic disorder to universal observable behavior, with a mature legacy across critical phenomena and quantum field theory. The proposed trio represents complementary foundational contributions to the physical formulation, exact structure, and broad application of the framework.",
    "case_against": "The intellectual lineage of conformal field theory is wide, and a three-person award inevitably compresses contributions by many theorists. Its experimental consequences are also less easily represented by a single canonical measurement than those of the leading instrumental candidates.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that B059 transformed correlated-electron theory into a quantitatively useful framework, with Georges, Vollhardt, and Kotliar forming a defensible foundational trio.",
        "response": "I agree that DMFT is an unusually mature and influential theoretical achievement and it is my strongest alternative. Yet its prize boundary is less sharp: the original conceptual method, later computational extensions, and materials-specific implementations are deeply intertwined. CFT more cleanly isolates a foundational universal mechanism and has a longer, more unified conceptual identity."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that B013 established geometric and topological quantum phases with broad, experimentally consequential effects over many decades.",
        "response": "I agree that the Aharonov–Bohm and Berry effects are fully mature and fundamental. My qualification is the same one Lindroth identifies: they are distinct historical and physical discoveries, so a joint citation risks joining achievements rather than recognizing one coherent advance. That attribution issue places B013 below B003 for me."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson stresses that B006 overcame the practical resolution limit of electron microscopy and has transformed materials physics through reproducible sub-angstrom imaging.",
        "response": "This is an exceptionally strong experimental alternative, especially because theory, instrumental realization, and scientific impact are all evident. I remain cautious because the credit chain extends through parallel correction approaches and many instrument-building teams; the historical boundary for three names requires more scrutiny than B003."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record supports this precise three-person compression of the broad development of conformal field theory.",
      "Whether the committee should favor a directly instrumental breakthrough over a foundational theoretical framework in this cycle."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No specific connection requiring recusal is apparent from the supplied profile. I share the Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but no specific collaboration or connection to a shortlisted achievement is supplied."
  }
}
